"candidate_profile_version": "2.0", "constraints_and_preferences": {"work_context": ["Mission-critical environments", "Highly regulated environments"], "communication": ["Strong written and verbal communication", "Cross-functional collaboration"], "resume_constraint": ["When describing current contracting work, clearly mark it as independent contractor work", "Avoid implying employee status where relevant"]}}	2026-03-09 06:09:53.54788+00	2026-03-13 19:26:56.813737+00	[CANDIDATE_LAYER_LEONID_VERMAN v1.0]\n\nCORE POSITIONING ANCHORS\n- Position as: Senior backend engineer + hands-on engineering leader (10–15+ years) specializing in high-performance, fault-tolerant distributed systems in fintech/crypto.\n- Emphasize: production-scale backend architecture under regulatory constraints; low-latency + correctness for risk-critical paths.\n- Anchor credibility with: built/launched crypto exchange for Japan; scaled to 1M+ users; top-5 (at times #1) by trading volume (state as provided; do not add dates or extra rankings).\n- Secondary positioning: AI/LLM model training & evaluation work as independent contractor; frame as applied evaluation + problem formulation, not “AI platform owner” unless evidence exists.\n\nARCHITECTURAL DIFFERENTIATION (MANDATORY SURFACING)\nSurface explicitly (as mechanisms) wherever role-relevant:\n- Horizontal scaling of trading servers to handle throughput growth.\n- Database read replicas to isolate read-heavy GET traffic from write workloads.\n- Multiple caching layers to reduce DB load and stabilize latency under peak traffic.\n- Asynchronous messaging to decouple components; event-driven architecture where supported.\n- Stateless service design to simplify scaling and deployment.\n- Safe account rebalancing and migration mechanisms (describe as operationally safe movement of accounts across servers/services).\n- Stateless identity propagation with centralized revocation via Redis (describe as distributed session validation pattern).\n\nARCHITECTURE SIGNAL PRIORITIZATION\n- Prefer bullets that include: replicas, caching layers, async messaging, stateless services, distributed session validation, safe migration/rebalancing.\n- Next: reliability/performance improvements tied to those mechanisms (no invented metrics).\n- Next: distributed systems tradeoffs (latency, correctness, fail-closed, regulatory constraints).\n- Then: leadership/process contributions (mentorship, standards, code review, decision-making).\n- Ensure at least one mechanism-heavy bullet appears in the most recent/most prominent role section if evidence exists.\n\nDISTRIBUTED SYSTEM DEPTH\n- Make distributed-systems mechanics explicit:\n  - traffic isolation (read replicas; caching tiers)\n  - decoupling (queues; async workflows)\n  - correctness/safety (idempotent processing; retry-safe design; fail-closed for risk-critical paths)\n  - statelessness (stateless services; stateless token propagation)\n  - operational rigor (incident response; observability mindset: metrics/logs/tracing)\n- Tie each mechanism to a concrete system area (trading servers, API layer, session validation, account migration) and a constraint (low latency, regulatory timeline, correctness).\n\nTECHNICAL DIFFERENTIATION\n- Primary stack signals to surface when supported by the source resume:\n  - Languages: Java, C#, Python, TypeScript (avoid implying all used in the same role unless evidence).\n  - Datastores: MySQL/PostgreSQL + Redis; Oracle if explicitly tied to a role.\n  - APIs: REST + WebSockets (use WebSockets specifically for real-time market/trading contexts only if supported).\n  - Infra: AWS, Docker, Kubernetes, CI/CD (do not claim ownership of infra platform unless supported).\n  - Async: Kafka/RabbitMQ + message queues; idempotent + retry-safe processing.\n- Prefer “mechanism-first” bullets (e.g., “Introduced read replicas…” not “Improved scalability…”).\n\nDOMAIN / INDUSTRY FRAMING\n- Treat crypto exchange experience as: high-throughput financial transaction platform with strict correctness, latency, and reliability constraints in a regulated environment.\n- Payments / settlement / risk controls framing: emphasize risk-critical decision paths, fail-closed behavior, auditability-minded engineering (without claiming specific compliance frameworks).\n- Avoid niche crypto jargon unless the job explicitly asks for it; keep the framing portable to fintech/payments.\n\nSAFE DOMAIN TRANSLATION\n- Safe translations:\n  - “Crypto exchange” → “high-throughput transaction platform / trading system with strict correctness constraints.”\n  - “Risk/margin controls” → “risk-critical validation and decisioning paths; fail-closed safety.”\n  - “Regulatory constraints” → “production safety constraints; controlled change management; limited architectural options (e.g., sharding constraints).”\n- Keep translations conservative; preserve “Japan market exchange launch” as factual context, not as a claim of regulatory/legal ownership.\n\nDOMAIN MISINTERPRETATION GUARDRAILS\n- Do NOT imply expertise/ownership in unrelated domains unless explicitly present in source evidence:\n  - unrelated SaaS business domains, asset discovery/asset management platforms\n  - networking protocol implementations\n  - low-code frameworks\n  - generic ETL/data integration platforms\n  - specific compliance framework implementation (SOC2/ISO/PCI/etc.) unless explicitly supported\n- Do NOT invent scale metrics beyond “1M+ users” and the provided ranking/volume statement.\n- Do NOT add employer names, dates, or jurisdictions beyond what is provided.\n\nEXTENSIBILITY AND INTEGRATION POSITIONING\n- When tailoring to “platform/extensibility/integration” roles, reinterpret existing evidence safely:\n  - async messaging → decoupled service communication contracts; integration via events/queues\n  - caching layers → performance isolation layer; consistent read strategy for read-heavy endpoints\n  - stateless services → modular service boundaries enabling horizontal scaling\n  - safe account migration/rebalancing → operational tooling/workflows for safe state movement across service boundaries\n- Avoid claiming “built an integration platform” unless the master resume explicitly supports it; keep it as “designed decoupled interfaces via queues/events.”\n\nLEADERSHIP FRAMING\n- Frame leadership as hands-on technical leadership:\n  - led teams up to 15–20; started from 4 engineers (use as scaling-of-team context)\n  - sets clarity/accountability; transparent decision-making\n  - code review as quality mechanism; mentorship and standards\n  - pushes back on risky changes; adds guardrails for regulated/mission-critical systems\n- Prefer leadership bullets that show mechanisms (e.g., “instituted review gates for risk-critical changes,” “defined operational runbooks,” “incident response ownership”) only if supported by source evidence.\n\nLEADERSHIP BALANCE FOR SENIOR ROLE\n- For senior IC roles: keep ~70–85% of bullets technical/architectural; leadership as supporting evidence.\n- For engineering lead/manager roles: still keep architecture depth prominent; leadership should reference delivery under constraints and production safety, not generic people-management claims.\n- Avoid vague leadership adjectives; use observable actions (mentored, reviewed, set standards, drove decisions, owned incidents) only when evidence exists.\n\nAI WORK POSITIONING\n- Must label current AI work as: “Independent contractor (via platform) supporting AI labs” (or equivalent wording).\n- Scope boundaries:\n  - Allowed: model training/evaluation support, creating ambiguous senior-level SWE tasks, evaluating reasoning robustness, rapid prototyping, A/B tests, familiarity with RAG/vector DB concepts, AI services integration familiarity.\n  - Not allowed unless evidenced: owning ML infrastructure, training proprietary foundation models end-to-end, deploying production ML platforms, being an employee of the lab.\n- Include AI tooling principles: engineer remains accountable; do not paste sensitive data; security/safety first (especially in regulated contexts).\n\nCLAIM BOUNDARIES / PROHIBITED INFLATION\n- Do not invent:\n  - metrics (latency, TPS, revenue, uptime), scale numbers beyond “1M+ users”\n  - specific compliance frameworks or protocol implementations\n  - employer names, titles, dates, or technologies not in evidence\n- Security/auth boundaries:\n  - Do NOT claim direct OAuth2/OIDC/SAML implementation.\n  - You MAY describe: encrypted signed session tokens; stateless identity propagation; expiry enforcement; centralized revocation via Redis; distributed session validation; RBAC-style API access control (as patterns, not standards compliance).\n- Employment boundary:\n  - AI-lab work must remain clearly “independent contractor”; never imply employee status.\n\nEVIDENCE SOURCES\n- Only construct bullets from:\n  - MASTER_RESUME (if provided at generation time)\n  - CANDIDATE_PROFILE.experience_highlights (including architecture_patterns, constraints_and_tradeoffs, security_auth_patterns, team_context, impact)\n  - CANDIDATE_PROFILE.technical_skills (patterns and named technologies)\n  - CANDIDATE_PROFILE.leadership (scope/practices/risk_management)\n  - CANDIDATE_PROFILE.ai_tooling_practice (tools, usage patterns, principles)\n- If a mechanism/technology is not explicitly present in those sources, do not introduce it.\n\nFINAL REMINDER\n- Core differentiation to keep dominant: mechanism-driven distributed backend architecture (replicas + caching + async decoupling + stateless services + safe migrations) proven at 1M+ user scale in regulated fintech/crypto, with hands-on technical leadership and clearly bounded AI evaluation contracting.	t
