rity_auth_patterns": [], "constraints_and_tradeoffs": []}, {"area": "Human Resources Management", "impact": ["Delivered reusable React/TypeScript components"], "market": null, "team_context": [], "skills_applied": [], "architecture_patterns": [], "employer_relationship": null, "security_auth_patterns": [], "constraints_and_tradeoffs": []}], "candidate_profile_version": "2.0", "constraints_and_preferences": {"work_context": [], "communication": [], "resume_constraint": []}}	2026-03-15 22:45:51.501925+00	2026-03-16 02:18:59.877099+00	[CANDIDATE_LAYER_OLI_TREADWELL v1.0]\n\nCORE POSITIONING ANCHORS\n- Position as: Senior Software Engineer (10+ years) specializing in modern web application development with strong front-end architecture and full-stack delivery.\n- Emphasize: building accessible, user-friendly interfaces; component-driven UI engineering; pragmatic collaboration across design/engineering.\n- Anchor stack: React + TypeScript + Node.js with Rails/PostgreSQL experience; testing discipline (Jest/RSpec) and UI system tooling (Storybook).\n- Prefer role targeting language: “Senior/Staff-level UI or Full-Stack Engineer (web)” rather than “distributed systems/backend platform” unless the job explicitly matches and evidence exists.\n\nARCHITECTURAL DIFFERENTIATION (MANDATORY SURFACING)\n- Surface explicitly (when relevant to the target role):\n  - Re-architected a Rewards platform from Rails-rendered UI to ReactJS (treat as a front-end architecture modernization; do not imply a full backend rewrite unless supported).\n  - Built/maintained a modular design system to drive UI consistency and reuse (component library + standards).\n  - Componentization and reuse: reusable React/TypeScript components for HR management workflows.\n- Ensure at least one bullet in the most recent/most prominent role includes a concrete architecture mechanism: “migrated UI architecture from Rails to React”, “introduced Storybook-driven component library”, “standardized design tokens/components”, etc.\n\nARCHITECTURE SIGNAL PRIORITIZATION\n- Prioritize bullets that show:\n  1) Concrete UI/platform architecture mechanisms (migration approach, modular component system, design system governance).\n  2) Quality mechanisms (testing strategy with Jest/Chai/RSpec; CI usage with CircleCI) tied to delivery reliability.\n  3) Cross-functional execution with design tools (Figma/Zeplin) only when tied to implementation mechanisms (handoff workflow, component specs).\n  4) Leadership (code review, delegation, coaching) as supporting evidence, not the main story.\n- Prefer rewriting achievements to expose the mechanism behind “15% engagement increase” (what changed in UI/UX architecture) without inventing details.\n\nDISTRIBUTED SYSTEM DEPTH\n- Do NOT force distributed-systems framing; only include if MASTER_RESUME provides explicit evidence (queues, caching, replicas, sharding, idempotency, tracing, etc.).\n- If a job description demands distributed systems, translate cautiously:\n  - Emphasize “web application architecture, API integration, performance-minded UI” rather than claiming backend scaling mechanisms not present in evidence.\n- Avoid terms like “high-throughput”, “low-latency”, “microservices”, “event-driven” unless explicitly supported by evidence sources.\n\nTECHNICAL DIFFERENTIATION\n- Emphasize concrete strengths supported by evidence:\n  - ReactJS + TypeScript UI architecture; component libraries; Storybook workflows.\n  - NodeJS and Ruby on Rails full-stack delivery; GraphQL usage (only as “worked with GraphQL” unless deeper ownership is evidenced).\n  - PostgreSQL via Rails/ActiveRecord (avoid claiming DBA-level work).\n  - Testing: Jest/Chai (JS) and RSpec (Rails); prefer mechanism-first quality bullets.\n  - CI: CircleCI (state as used/maintained pipelines only if evidenced).\n- Prefer mechanism-first bullet phrasing:\n  - “Introduced Storybook-based component documentation to standardize UI patterns…” (only if supported).\n  - “Migrated Rails-rendered views to React components to enable modular UI development…” (safe given architecture_patterns).\n\nDOMAIN / INDUSTRY FRAMING\n- Primary domain credibility: Human Resources software, rewards/engagement platforms, education-related web products; secondary exposure to museums.\n- Frame domains as product contexts, not specialized compliance/regulatory expertise.\n- Prefer: “HR management workflows” / “rewards engagement UI” rather than niche HR/legal claims.\n\nSAFE DOMAIN TRANSLATION\n- Translate domain experience to broadly applicable web-product value:\n  - HR management → complex internal/admin workflows, forms, permissions-sensitive UI (do not claim auth implementation unless evidenced).\n  - Rewards platform → consumer-facing engagement features and UI iteration tied to measurable engagement outcomes.\n  - Education/museums → content-rich experiences and usability/accessibility emphasis (only if supported by MASTER_RESUME).\n- Keep translations conservative: focus on UI architecture, component reuse, and delivery practices.\n\nDOMAIN MISINTERPRETATION GUARDRAILS\n- Do NOT claim specialization in:\n  - Security/compliance frameworks (SOC2, HIPAA, GDPR) unless explicitly in evidence.\n  - Deep backend/platform engineering (microservices, Kubernetes, Kafka, Redis, etc.) unless explicitly in evidence.\n  - Data engineering/ETL pipelines or ML/AI systems.\n- Do NOT invent scale metrics (traffic, users, latency) or migration details (e.g., “strangler pattern”, “micro-frontend”) unless present in evidence.\n\nEXTENSIBILITY AND INTEGRATION POSITIONING\n- Reinterpret “design system” and “reusable components” as extensibility signals:\n  - “Modular component architecture enabling faster feature delivery and consistent UI.”\n  - “Shared UI primitives/patterns used across multiple product areas.”\n- For integration language, stay within evidence:\n  - It is safe to mention “GraphQL integration” only as usage; do not claim schema ownership, federation, or gateway architecture unless supported.\n- Emphasize maintainability mechanisms: component boundaries, consistent styling (SASS/SCSS), testing, and review standards.\n\nLEADERSHIP FRAMING\n- Frame leadership as hands-on technical leadership:\n  - Delegated ownership of components/features; set expectations via code review.\n  - Mentored/coached junior engineers through 1:1s and feedback loops.\n  - Protected production stability by pushing back on risky changes; describe as “risk-based decisioning” without dramatization.\n- Prefer leadership evidence tied to mechanisms:\n  - “Established code review norms for React/TypeScript components…” (only if supported).\n  - “Coached juniors on testing practices (Jest/RSpec)…” (only if supported).\n\nLEADERSHIP BALANCE FOR SENIOR ROLE\n- Keep narrative execution-heavy:\n  - 70–85% technical architecture + delivery bullets; 15–30% leadership/process.\n- Avoid sounding like an engineering manager unless the target role is EM and evidence supports it.\n- Leadership should reinforce impact (quality, maintainability, delivery speed), not replace technical depth.\n\nAI WORK POSITIONING\n- No AI tooling or AI project evidence is provided.\n- Do NOT mention AI/LLMs, prompt engineering, model evaluation, or AI-driven features unless present in MASTER_RESUME or explicitly added evidence.\n\nCLAIM BOUNDARIES / PROHIBITED INFLATION\n- Do not claim:\n  - Backend re-architecture beyond “UI/front-end migration from Rails to React” unless evidence shows backend changes.\n  - Security/auth implementation, compliance, or privacy programs (none evidenced).\n  - DevOps/infra ownership (no infra stack evidenced).\n  - Observability systems (no metrics/logging/tracing tooling evidenced).\n- Do not fabricate:\n  - Additional metrics beyond “15% increase in customer engagement” and “delivered reusable components.”\n  - Team size, timelines, budgets, or scope.\n- When referencing tools (Figma/Zeplin/JIRA/GitHub), keep them as collaboration/tooling context, not primary achievements.\n\nEVIDENCE SOURCES\n- Only use:\n  - MASTER_RESUME (if provided at generation time)\n  - CANDIDATE_PROFILE.summary/headline\n  - CANDIDATE_PROFILE.experience_highlights (areas, impacts, skills_applied, architecture_patterns)\n  - CANDIDATE_PROFILE.technical_skills\n  - CANDIDATE_PROFILE.leadership (practices + risk_management)\n- Bullet construction rules:\n  - Every mechanism must be traceable to an evidence source above.\n  - If a mechanism is not explicitly evidenced, phrase conservatively (“worked with”, “contributed to”) or omit.\n\nFINAL REMINDER\n- Core differentiation to keep visible: senior-level React/TypeScript UI architecture + design-system-driven component reuse, with full-stack fluency (Node/Rails/Postgres) and pragmatic technical leadership focused on quality and stability.	t
