"candidate_profile_version": "2.0", "constraints_and_preferences": {"work_context": ["Mission-critical environments", "Highly regulated environments"], "communication": ["Strong written and verbal communication", "Cross-functional collaboration"], "resume_constraint": ["When describing current contracting work, clearly mark it as independent contractor work", "Avoid implying employee status where relevant"]}}	2026-03-18 05:02:36.578398+00	2026-03-18 05:39:20.648077+00	[CANDIDATE_LAYER_LEONID_VERMAN v1.0]\n\nCORE POSITIONING ANCHORS\n- Position as: Senior backend engineer + hands-on engineering leader (10–15+ years) specializing in high-performance, fault-tolerant distributed systems in fintech/crypto.\n- Emphasize: production-scale backend architecture under regulatory constraints; low-latency + correctness for risk-critical paths.\n- Anchor credibility with: built/launched crypto exchange for Japan; scaled to 1M+ users; top-5 (at times #1) by trading volume (state as provided; do not embellish).\n- Secondary positioning: pragmatic technical leader (teams up to 15–20) who sets standards, mentors, and protects production stability.\n- AI positioning: independent contractor supporting AI labs via model training/evaluation on senior SWE tasks; not “AI platform owner” unless explicitly evidenced.\n\nARCHITECTURAL DIFFERENTIATION (MANDATORY SURFACING)\n- Always surface explicit mechanisms from the exchange work when writing/rewriting bullets:\n  - Horizontal scaling of trading servers.\n  - Database read replicas to isolate read-heavy GET traffic.\n  - Multiple caching layers to reduce DB load.\n  - Asynchronous messaging to decouple components.\n  - Stateless service design.\n  - Safe account rebalancing and migration.\n- Also surface security/auth architecture mechanisms (without overstating standards/frameworks):\n  - Encrypted signed session tokens.\n  - Distributed session validation via Redis.\n  - Stateless identity propagation + centralized revocation.\n  - Account context extraction from token.\n  - API-level access control (RBAC-style).\n\nARCHITECTURE SIGNAL PRIORITIZATION\n- When selecting bullets for tailoring, prioritize in this order:\n  1) Concrete architecture mechanisms listed above (replicas/caching/async/stateless/scaling/migration).\n  2) Reliability/correctness mechanisms for risk-critical paths (fail-closed, validation, revocation).\n  3) Distributed systems tradeoffs under regulatory constraints (why certain sharding/partitioning was harder).\n  4) Observability/incident response mechanisms (metrics/logs/tracing mindset; incident response ownership if evidenced).\n  5) Leadership/process contributions (mentorship, code review standards, decision-making).\n- Ensure at least one mechanism-heavy bullet appears in the most recent relevant backend role section (or equivalent) whenever evidence exists.\n\nDISTRIBUTED SYSTEM DEPTH\n- For backend bullets, explicitly include distributed-system mechanics and constraints:\n  - Traffic isolation (read replicas; caching layers).\n  - Decoupling (queues; async messaging).\n  - Stateless services and token-based identity propagation.\n  - Idempotent processing + retry-safe design (only if tied to queues/Kafka/RabbitMQ usage).\n  - Operational safety for risk-critical paths (fail-closed behavior; validation latency constraints).\n- Prefer phrasing that shows tradeoffs and boundaries:\n  - “Isolated read-heavy endpoints from write workloads via read replicas…”\n  - “Decoupled matching/validation/settlement-adjacent components via async messaging…”\n- Avoid generic statements like “built scalable microservices” unless immediately followed by the concrete mechanism(s).\n\nTECHNICAL DIFFERENTIATION\n- Treat as primary stack signals (only when relevant to the job description):\n  - Languages: Java, C#, Python, TypeScript (VBA only if explicitly relevant).\n  - Datastores: MySQL, PostgreSQL, Redis, Oracle.\n  - API patterns: REST, WebSockets (use WebSockets specifically for real-time exchange-style contexts if supported).\n  - Infra: AWS, Docker, Kubernetes, CI/CD.\n  - Async: Kafka, RabbitMQ; message queues; idempotent + retry-safe design.\n  - Observability: metrics/logs/tracing mindset (do not invent specific tools).\n- Convert “high-throughput/low-latency” into mechanism-first bullets (replicas/caching/async/stateless validation), not adjectives.\n\nDOMAIN / INDUSTRY FRAMING\n- Primary domain credibility: fintech + crypto exchange infrastructure + regulated systems.\n- Frame crypto exchange work as:\n  - high-throughput financial transaction platform\n  - strict correctness and safety constraints\n  - regulatory-driven delivery constraints (timeline, architecture limitations)\n- Secondary domain themes to surface when relevant:\n  - payments/settlement-adjacent thinking (only if supported by the role text; do not invent payment rails)\n  - risk and margin controls as “risk-critical decisioning paths” and “fail-closed validation” (do not claim specific financial models unless evidenced).\n\nSAFE DOMAIN TRANSLATION\n- Allowed conservative translations:\n  - “Crypto exchange” → “high-throughput transaction platform with strict correctness constraints.”\n  - “Risk/margin controls” → “risk-critical validation and authorization paths; fail-closed behavior.”\n  - “Regulatory constraints” → “safety, auditability, and delivery constraints affecting architecture choices.”\n  - “Trading servers” → “low-latency backend services handling high request volume and correctness-sensitive operations.”\n- If targeting non-crypto fintech roles, emphasize mechanisms (scaling, caching, replicas, async decoupling, stateless auth) over crypto-specific terminology.\n\nDOMAIN MISINTERPRETATION GUARDRAILS\n- Do NOT claim or imply:\n  - Implementation of OAuth2/OIDC/SAML or being an “identity provider” implementer.\n  - Specific compliance frameworks (SOC2, ISO, PCI, etc.) unless explicitly provided elsewhere.\n  - Ownership of unrelated SaaS business domains or product categories not in evidence.\n  - Blockchain protocol implementation, consensus engineering, or core cryptography research (unless explicitly evidenced).\n  - Specific observability stacks (Prometheus, Datadog, etc.) unless explicitly evidenced.\n- Do NOT invent scale metrics beyond what is provided (1M+ users; top-5/#1 by volume at times). No fabricated latency/throughput numbers.\n\nEXTENSIBILITY AND INTEGRATION POSITIONING\n- When a role calls for extensibility/integration architecture, safely reinterpret existing evidence as:\n  - Stateless services + token-based identity → “portable auth context propagation across services.”\n  - Redis-backed session validation/revocation → “centralized control plane for distributed authorization decisions.”\n  - Async messaging → “integration-friendly decoupling layer between components.”\n  - Multi-layer caching + read replicas → “performance isolation layers protecting core databases.”\n  - Safe account rebalancing/migration → “operational tooling and workflows for live system evolution without breaking auth/correctness.”\n- Keep claims grounded: describe patterns and boundaries; do not claim “built an integration platform” unless explicitly supported.\n\nLEADERSHIP FRAMING\n- Frame leadership as hands-on technical leadership:\n  - Led teams up to 15–20 in building/scaling the exchange (state team size as provided).\n  - Emphasize: clear task definition, transparent decisions, collaborative code review, mentorship/standards.\n  - Include risk management stance: pushes back on risky changes; adds guardrails for regulated/mission-critical systems.\n- Prefer leadership bullets that include an observable mechanism:\n  - “Established code review standards + release guardrails to reduce production risk…”\n  - “Defined ownership boundaries and on-call/incident response expectations…” (only if incident response ownership is evidenced in source material).\n\nLEADERSHIP BALANCE FOR SENIOR ROLE\n- For senior IC / staff-level backend roles:\n  - Keep 60–80% of bullets technical/architectural (mechanism-first).\n  - Leadership appears as multiplier: decision-making, standards, mentoring, delivery under constraints.\n  - Avoid drifting into people-management-only narrative; keep “built/introduced/designed” verbs prominent.\n\nAI WORK POSITIONING\n- Must label AI-lab work as: “Independent contractor (via platform)” and keep scope precise:\n  - Model training/evaluation support; creating ambiguous senior SWE tasks; evaluating robustness/reasoning.\n  - Tool usage (Cursor/ChatGPT/Claude Code/OpenAI API) as productivity and evaluation tooling, not as “built foundation models.”\n  - Mention RAG/vector DB familiarity only as familiarity unless there is evidence of production implementation.\n- Do not imply employment at specific AI labs; do not name clients unless explicitly provided elsewhere.\n- If job requires AI integration: position as “AI services integration familiarity + safe usage practices,” not “ML engineer.”\n\nCLAIM BOUNDARIES / PROHIBITED INFLATION\n- Do not claim:\n  - OAuth2/OIDC/SAML implementation; instead say “JWT-style stateless tokens with Redis-backed validation/revocation” (as evidenced).\n  - Specific compliance framework implementation or audits.\n  - Unstated metrics (latency, TPS, revenue impact, cost savings).\n  - Unstated technologies (service mesh, specific cloud services, specific monitoring tools).\n  - Unstated domain ownership (payments rails, banking core systems, DeFi protocol engineering).\n- Keep “blockchain” references conservative unless the bullet is explicitly about exchange infrastructure; avoid implying protocol-level blockchain engineering.\n- Contractor constraint: never imply AI work is full-time employment; always mark as independent contractor.\n\nEVIDENCE SOURCES\n- Only construct bullets from:\n  - MASTER_RESUME (if provided at generation time)\n  - CANDIDATE_PROFILE.experience_highlights (including architecture_patterns, constraints_and_tradeoffs, security_auth_patterns)\n  - CANDIDATE_PROFILE.technical_skills (including scalability_reliability_patterns, security_auth_patterns, async_messaging)\n  - CANDIDATE_PROFILE.leadership (scope/practices/risk_management)\n  - CANDIDATE_PROFILE.claim_boundaries and constraints_and_preferences (for wording constraints)\n- If a mechanism/metric is not in these sources, do not introduce it.\n\nFINAL REMINDER\n- Core differentiation to keep explicit: built and scaled regulated, high-throughput fintech/crypto exchange backend using concrete distributed-systems mechanisms (horizontal scaling, read replicas, multi-layer caching, async decoupling, stateless auth with Redis-backed validation/revocation) while leading teams hands-on—plus clearly labeled AI model training/evaluation contractor experience.	t
